  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two solar-driven water-treatment reactor designs. On the left, a composite parabolic collector uses a curved reflective surface to concentrate sunlight onto a central tubular photoreactor. Fluid enters one end of the tube, passes through the illuminated section where the light is focused, and exits from the other end. The drawing emphasizes the optical role of the reflector and the linear flow through the tube.\n\nOn the right, a raceway pond reactor is shown as a shallow oval channel with circulating flow, indicated by arrows moving around the loop. Effluent and catalysts are fed into the system through an inlet pipe, apparently near the center, while treated water leaves through an outlet pipe. The layout suggests continuous mixing and recirculation in an open pond-like basin rather than in a closed tube.\n\nTaken together, the image highlights two contrasting photoreactor configurations for treatment: a more concentrated, enclosed tubular system that maximizes sunlight capture with mirrors, and a larger open raceway system that relies on circulation and contact between wastewater, catalysts, and light. The main comparison is therefore between focused-light tubular processing and mixed open-pond processing.",
